@node LibreClaw Usage
@section Usage

@subsection One-command entry point

If you just want to start chatting:

@example
M-x cmacs-libreclaw-open
@end example

On first run this will:

@enumerate
@item
Ensure libreclaw is running --- auto-generating a default config
at @code{cmacs-libreclaw-default-workspace} if one is not already
configured.
@item
Detect the current project directory via @code{project-current},
@code{vc-root-dir}, or @code{locate-dominating-file} for common
markers (@file{.git}, @file{Cargo.toml}, @file{pyproject.toml},
etc.).  Falls back to the current @code{default-directory} when
no project is detected.
@item
Open a dedicated org-mode buffer named
@samp{*libreclaw: <project>*} bound to that project root.  The
first time you open a project, libreclaw starts a fresh AI
session for it.  Subsequent opens restore the conversation from
disk (@file{~/.libreclaw/cmacs-sessions/<sha>.org}) and continue
the same session.
@end enumerate

Two different projects, two different buffers, two independent
conversations.  The cmacs channel gives each project root its
own room key, so libreclaw's session manager keeps their memory
separated automatically:

@example
M-x find-file ~/Documents/notes
M-x cmacs-libreclaw-open
  -> *libreclaw: notes*     (fresh session for the notes project)

M-x find-file ~/source/projects/cmacs
M-x cmacs-libreclaw-open
  -> *libreclaw: cmacs*     (completely separate session)
@end example

Both buffers persist across Emacs restarts.

@subsection Starting and stopping manually

@code{cmacs-libreclaw-open} handles the lifecycle for you, but
you can drive it directly:

@example
(setq cmacs-libreclaw-config-file "~/.libreclaw/config.yaml")
M-x cmacs-libreclaw-start
@end example

Calling @code{cmacs-libreclaw-start} when it is already running
signals @code{cmacs-libreclaw-error}.  Use
@code{cmacs-libreclaw-running-p} to check first.

Stop with @kbd{M-x cmacs-libreclaw-stop}.  Room buffers are left in
place with their history preserved; killing them removes them from
@code{cmacs-libreclaw-rooms-alist}.

@subsection Room buffers

Each room surfaces as a buffer named
@samp{*libreclaw:CHANNEL-ID:ROOM-ID*}.  The mode is
@code{cmacs-libreclaw-room-mode}, derived from @code{org-mode}, with
a fixed structure:

@example
#+TITLE: LibreClaw — <room-name>
#+STARTUP: showall indent
#+OPTIONS: toc:nil num:nil
#+PROPERTY: channel <channel-id>
#+PROPERTY: room-id <room-id>

* Messages

** 2026-04-15 14:30:12  bob
Hey, just checking in.

** 2026-04-15 14:31:05  you
All good — working on the libreclaw integration.

* Compose                                             :compose:
<cursor parks here; type, then C-c C-c sends>
@end example

Everything above the @samp{* Compose} sentinel is read-only.  The
region below is a normal org buffer — source blocks, tables, links,
lists, footnotes all work as usual.

@subsection Sending a message

Type below @samp{* Compose} and press @kbd{C-c C-c}.  The compose
region is read, cleared, sent async through the libreclaw channel,
and inserted as a new @samp{** TIMESTAMP you} heading above the
sentinel as a local echo.

Buffer-local bindings:

@table @kbd
@item C-c C-c
@code{cmacs-libreclaw-send-compose}
@item C-c C-k
@code{cmacs-libreclaw-clear-compose}
@item C-c C-x C-f
@code{cmacs-libreclaw-attach-file}
@item C-c C-a
@code{cmacs-ai-view-attach} --- attach what you have on screen
@end table

@kbd{RET}, @kbd{TAB}, @kbd{C-c C-l} are standard org-mode keys and
work as usual for multi-line editing.

@subsection Why the transcript cannot be edited

A read-only region in Emacs has two ways to fail @emph{open} ---
protecting nothing while looking protected --- and cmacs hit both.

@enumerate
@item
@strong{Emacs clears @code{before-change-functions} when a function on
it signals.}  A guard that signals @code{text-read-only} therefore
protects a buffer @emph{exactly once}: after the first stray keypress
the hook is gone and every edit after it lands.  That is what the
transcript guard used to be on its own.

@item
@strong{@code{(read-only t rear-nonsticky t)} --- the comint idiom ---
permits insertion in the MIDDLE of the run.}  With @code{rear-nonsticky}
Emacs decides inserted text would join neither neighbouring interval and
allows the insert, so only the run's edges were protected.
@end enumerate

So finished text carries @code{(read-only t front-sticky t)}, applied at
each delivered message; the property is enforced in C and cannot be disarmed.
The change guard is kept for anything not yet sealed, and is
re-armed every time the transcript is sealed, so a clearing heals itself
at the next message rather than lasting the session.

Both are covered by tests that try the same edit three times and once in
the middle --- a single attempt at the edge passes against either bug.

@subsection What the agent is told about your screen

An agent room is a chat with something that can drive your editor, so
``summarize this'' has to mean the buffer you are looking at rather
than the room you typed it in.  Each outgoing message therefore carries
an invisible prelude: a listing of the buffers currently on your screen
--- name, file, mode, where point is, which lines are visible --- and,
on the first message of a room, a standing explanation of what that
listing is for.

@strong{The room shows exactly what you typed.}  The prelude rides the
wire and nothing else; the local echo and the send are separate
statements in @code{cmacs-libreclaw-send-compose} for that reason, and
there is a test holding them apart.  A transcript of an agent
conversation is still readable a week later.

The listing carries no buffer text.  The agent has cmacs's whole MCP
surface tunnelled to it, so it reads what it needs with
@code{get_buffer_content}, @code{search_buffer} or
@code{current_view}.  @xref{CMacs AI View}, for the full model, the
exclusion rules, and the @code{inline} fallback for an agent with no
tools.

@strong{Only cmacs's own agent rooms.}  A matrix room, a mailing list
or a webhook channel is other people, and what is on your screen is
none of their business --- the branch in
@code{cmacs-libreclaw-send-compose} is what enforces that.

@multitable @columnfractions .34 .26 .40
@headitem Variable @tab Default @tab What
@item @code{cmacs-libreclaw-view-mode} @tab @code{tools}
  @tab @code{tools} (agent has MCP) or @code{inline}
@item @code{cmacs-libreclaw-context-function}
  @tab @code{cmacs-libreclaw-default-context}
  @tab build the prelude yourself, or nil to send nothing
@end multitable

Set @code{cmacs-libreclaw-view-mode} to @code{inline} if the agent
keeps saying it cannot see your screen --- that means its MCP config
does not include the cmacs server.  @code{cmacs-ai-view-attach-mode}
set to @code{off} disables the whole thing here too.

@kbd{C-c C-a} forces the payload: the visible buffers' actual text,
dropped into the compose region where you can trim it before sending.

@subsection Switching between rooms

@kbd{M-x cmacs-libreclaw-show-room} prompts with a completion list
of @samp{CHANNEL/ROOM-ID} strings.

@subsection Sending programmatically

@example
(cmacs-libreclaw-send-message
 "matrix" "!roomid:example.com"
 "Hello from Elisp!")
@end example

Optional @var{HTML-BODY} and @var{THREAD-ID} arguments cover Matrix
reply threads.
